{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794347130168,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c76c5d27001VvwJ5U3xf0Iu0r","messageID":"msg_1c76c3bd9001DnzL1sJgna4dbR","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794347130168,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c76c5d2a001qz2csQqZSAFmzF","messageID":"msg_1c76c3bd9001DnzL1sJgna4dbR","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"text","text":"<think>Wait, there's an issue with `parse_amount`: `int(value.scaleb(2).to_integral_value(rounding=\"ROUND_HALF_UP\"))` — `to_integral_value` accepts rounding as a string? It accepts a rounding mode constant (ROUND_HALF_UP is the string \"ROUND_HALF_UP\" from decimal module, which is a string constant). Yes, `decimal.ROUND_HALF_UP` == 'ROUND_HALF_UP' string. `to_integral_value(rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)` works since the module accepts string rounding modes. Let me verify in Python.\n\nAlso `Decimal(cents).scaleb(-2)` gives the exact 2dp value. For tolerance check: value=10.555 → cents = 1056 (10.56 * 100 = 1055.5 → to_integral rounds to 1056? 10.555 * 100 = 1055.5 → ROUND_HALF_UP → 1056 → 10.56). diff = |10.555 - 10.56| = 0.005 > 1e-6 → error. Good.\n\nvalue=0.1 → cents=10 → exact=10.0? Decimal(\"0.1\") vs Decimal(10).scaleb(-2) = Decimal(\"1.0\")? Hmm: Decimal(10).scaleb(-2) → Decimal('0.1'). Yes scaleb shifts the exponent: 10 → 1E+1 → 1E-1 → \"0.1\". Good, matches exactly.\n\nBut careful: Decimal(10).scaleb(-2) = Decimal('0.1') vs Decimal('0.10')? Decimal('0.1') == Decimal('0.10') → True (numeric equality). Good.\n\nAnother check: value = 100 → cents = 10000 → Decimal(10000).scaleb(-2) = Decimal('100.00')? 10000 → 1E+4 → 1E+2 = 100 → \"100\". equal to Decimal('100'). Good.\n\nNow `exact` check: `value == Decimal(cents).scaleb(-2)`. For a string input \"25.00\" → Decimal('25.00') == Decimal(2500).scaleb(-2)=Decimal('25') → True numerically. Good.\n\nNow the `to_integral_value` on `value.scaleb(2)`: for value = Decimal('0.001') → 0.1 → rounds to 0 → cents=0 → \"Amount must be greater than zero\". Good.\n\nNow `parse_amount` raises inside `transaction(db)` in create_transfer — the exception propagates out of the `with transaction` block → ROLLBACK → then the error handler catches ApiError. Good, since ApiError is raised inside the `with` block, the context manager rolls back and re-raises. ✓\n\nOne issue: in `create_transfer` I raise ApiError(404, \"Recipient not found\") inside the transaction — rollback happens, fine.\n\nBut: `with transaction(db)` where db.execute(\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE\") - if a transaction is already active (autocommit mode means no implicit transaction), fine.\n\nNow `app.py` — create the app, register the blueprint, error handlers, and serve the frontend.\n\nAlso need to handle: API 404 for unknown /api routes (JSON, not the SPA index). The catch-all frontend route `@app.get(\"/<path:path>\")` would catch /api/unknown and return index.html. Better: register the API blueprint with url_prefix /api and add an error handler for 404 that returns JSON if the path starts with /api. Let me handle in app.py:\n\n```python\n@app.errorhandler(404)\ndef not_found(e):\n    if request.path.startswith(\"/api/\"):\n        return error_response(404, \"Not found\")\n    return send index.html\n```\n\nBut the catch-all route handles all unmatched paths, so Flask's 404 for unknown API paths won't trigger unless the catch-all excludes /api. The catch-all `/<path:path>` will match \"api/foo\" too. So I should make the catch-all not match /api paths: check in the frontend route: if path starts with \"api/\" → return JSON 404.\n\nLet me restructure app.py:\n\n```python\n@app.get(\"/\")\n@app.get(\"/<path:path>\")\ndef frontend(path=\"index.html\"):\n    if path.startswith(\"api/\"):\n        return error_response(404, \"Not found\")\n    ...\n```\n\nAlso, the SPA needs to serve index.html for unknown routes. ✓ (already there).\n\nAlso add proper caching headers for static assets? Not needed.\n\nAlso register error handlers for ApiError and generic exceptions (500 → JSON for API paths).\n\nLet me write app.py.\n\nAdditionally: the frontend dist may not exist during development (backend run locally without a build). `send_from_directory` would 404. I'll handle gracefully: if the file doesn't exist, return a simple message.\n\nAlso serve `frontend/dist/assets/...` files — the catch-all handles them since they're files under DIST. ✓\n\nLet me write it.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794347130154,"end":1794347130165}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794347130191,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_01a1c76c54fa773f9099b0c8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"Flask entry point.\n\nServes the JSON API under /api and the built React frontend from the same\norigin.  Persistent data lives in ./data (SQLite).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom flask import Flask, request, send_from_directory\n\nfrom bank import db\nfrom bank.api import ApiError, api, error_response\n\nDIST = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / \"frontend\" / \"dist\"\n\napp = Flask(__name__, static_folder=None)\napp.config[\"BANK_DATA_DIR\"] = db.DATA_DIR\napp.json.sort_keys = False\n\napp.register_blueprint(api)\napp.teardown_appcontext(db.close_db)\n\n\n@app.errorhandler(ApiError)\ndef handle_api_error(error: ApiError):\n    return error_response(error.status, error.message)\n\n\n@app.errorhandler(404)\ndef handle_not_found(_error):\n    if request.path.startswith(\"/api\"):\n        return error_response(404, \"Not found\")\n    return serve_index()\n\n\n@app.errorhandler(Exception)\ndef handle_unexpected_error(error: Exception):\n    if isinstance(error, ApiError):\n        return error_response(error.status, error.message)\n    app.logger.exception(\"Unhandled error: %s\", error)\n    return error_response(500, \"Internal server error\")\n\n\n@app.get(\"/\")\n@app.get(\"/<path:path>\")\ndef frontend(path: str = \"index.html\"):\n    if path.startswith(\"api/\"):\n        return error_response(404, \"Not found\")\n    if path and (DIST / path).is_file():\n        return send_from_directory(DIST, path)\n    return serve_index()\n\n\ndef serve_index():\n    index = DIST / \"index.html\"\n    if not index.is_file():\n        return error_response(\n            404, \"Frontend build not found; run 'npm run build' in frontend/\"\n        )\n    return send_from_directory(DIST, \"index.html\")\n\n\ndef init() -> None:\n    \"\"\"Prepare the database before the app starts serving requests.\"\"\"\n    db.init_db()\n\n\ninit()\n","filePath":"/workspace/backend/app.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/workspace/backend/app.py","exists":true,"truncated":false},"title":"backend/app.py","time":{"start":1794347130162,"end":1794347130173}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c76c5d2d001L9Vgq6lfmJbFpk","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","messageID":"msg_1c76c3bd9001DnzL1sJgna4dbR"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794347130191,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c76c5d40001yzPwU81EXjFhy6","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_1c76c3bd9001DnzL1sJgna4dbR","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":31223,"input":3993,"output":455,"reasoning":1075,"cache":{"write":0,"read":25700}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794347146517,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c76c9d04001uY7WnLaZo61rmH","messageID":"msg_1c76c5d49001fInTJ9IIaWzgzy","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794347146518,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c76c9d06001hMn1bwH0C0xDWD","messageID":"msg_1c76c5d49001fInTJ9IIaWzgzy","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"text","text":"<think>Wait: `app.errorhandler(Exception)` will also catch HTTPException subclasses like 405 Method Not Allowed, converting them to 500 JSON. That's bad: a POST to a GET-only endpoint should be 405, and OPTIONS/HEAD handling. Let me handle HTTPException specially by re-raising / returning its own response.\n\n```python\nfrom werkzeug.exceptions import HTTPException\n\n@app.errorhandler(Exception)\ndef handle_unexpected_error(error):\n    if isinstance(error, ApiError): ...\n    if isinstance(error, HTTPException):\n        return error_response(error.code, error.description)\n    ...\n```\n\nAlso, the generic Exception handler registered for all exceptions in Flask — with `app.errorhandler(Exception)`, Flask will call it for HTTPException too unless handled. Let me add the HTTPException branch.\n\nAlso: `db.close_db` teardown — teardown_appcontext passes the exception. ✓\n\nAlso `db.DATA_DIR` is a relative path \"./data\" resolved relative to CWD (/app in Docker). ✓\n\nOne issue: `app.json.sort_keys = False` keeps key order as inserted. Fine.\n\nAnother: `handle_not_found` returns serve_index() for non-API paths — but the catch-all route means 404 rarely happens for non-API paths. Fine.\n\nAlso I should double check: the `/api` blueprint's `error_response` helper is imported from bank.api. ✓\n\nLet me also add a `favicon.ico`? Not needed; the catch-all returns index.html for it. Fine.\n\nNow let me update app.py for theHTTPException handling.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794347146502,"end":1794347146515}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794347146545,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_01a1c76c994f76459b0a8d14","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/backend/app.py","newString":"@app.errorhandler(Exception)\ndef handle_unexpected_error(error: Exception):\n    if isinstance(error, ApiError):\n        return error_response(error.status, error.message)\n    if isinstance(error, HTTPException):\n        return error_response(error.code or 500, error.description or \"Error\")\n    app.logger.exception(\"Unhandled error: %s\", error)\n    return error_response(500, \"Internal server error\")","oldString":"@app.errorhandler(Exception)\ndef handle_unexpected_error(error: Exception):\n    if isinstance(error, ApiError):\n        return error_response(error.status, error.message)\n    app.logger.exception(\"Unhandled error: %s\", error)\n    return error_response(500, \"Internal server error\")"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /workspace/backend/app.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/backend/app.py\n+++ /workspace/backend/app.py\n@@ -38,8 +38,10 @@\n @app.errorhandler(Exception)\n def handle_unexpected_error(error: Exception):\n     if isinstance(error, ApiError):\n         return error_response(error.status, error.message)\n+    if isinstance(error, HTTPException):\n+        return error_response(error.code or 500, error.description or \"Error\")\n     app.logger.exception(\"Unhandled error: %s\", error)\n     return error_response(500, \"Internal server error\")\n \n \n","filediff":{"file":"/workspace/backend/app.py","patch":"Index: /workspace/backend/app.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/backend/app.py\n+++ /workspace/backend/app.py\n@@ -38,8 +38,10 @@\n @app.errorhandler(Exception)\n def handle_unexpected_error(error: Exception):\n     if isinstance(error, ApiError):\n         return error_response(error.status, error.message)\n+    if isinstance(error, HTTPException):\n+        return error_response(error.code or 500, error.description or \"Error\")\n     app.logger.exception(\"Unhandled error: %s\", error)\n     return error_response(500, \"Internal server error\")\n \n \n","additions":2,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"backend/app.py","time":{"start":1794347146512,"end":1794347146525}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c76c9d09001ewHd5t3adiOxP6","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","messageID":"msg_1c76c5d49001fInTJ9IIaWzgzy"}}
