{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794347243555,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c76e1805001xr9pgE1ns5lyrF","messageID":"msg_1c76df702001XL7d2K2NK55eLS","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794347243555,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c76e1808001QBj5VJAZ4Q1azk","messageID":"msg_1c76df702001XL7d2K2NK55eLS","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"text","text":"<think>All tests pass, including the concurrency/atomicity test.\n\nNow the frontend. Let me plan the components:\n\n`frontend/src/index.css` — Tailwind entry:\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n```\nMaybe also a `@theme` for custom colors. Keep it minimal but nice. The instructions say: \"avoid hand-written CSS files except for Tailwind's own entry file\" — so index.css is allowed as the Tailwind entry. I'll add `@import \"tailwindcss\";` and optionally some base styles via @layer (that's still Tailwind entry file, allowed).\n\nFiles:\n- src/main.tsx — entry, import \"./index.css\"\n- src/App.tsx — main app\n- src/api.ts — API client\n- src/types.ts — types\n- src/components/LoginView.tsx (auth panel with tabs)\n- src/components/Dashboard.tsx? Maybe keep App as the orchestrator with subcomponents:\n  - components/AuthPanel.tsx\n  - components/BalanceCard.tsx\n  - components/TransferForm.tsx (with confirm dialog)\n  - components/TransactionHistory.tsx\n\nLet me design the UI:\n\nSigned-out view: centered card, \"Welcome to Meridian Bank\" (or some name), tabs for Sign in / Create account, username + password fields, submit button, error message area.\n\nSigned-in view:\n- Header bar: bank name/logo left, username + Sign out right.\n- Balance card prominently: big number \"1,000.00\" with currency, label \"Available balance\".\n- Grid: left column = transfer form; right column = transaction history (or history below on mobile). I'll do a 2-col layout on lg screens: form (1/3) + history (2/3). Actually balance prominent at top full width.\n\nTransfer form: recipient username input, amount input (type=number, step 0.01), memo input, \"Review transfer\" button → confirmation modal showing details → \"Confirm\" / \"Cancel\". On confirm → POST; on error show API error message inline (e.g., \"Insufficient funds: your balance is too low for this transfer\"). On success → show success message, reset the form, refresh balance and history.\n\nTransaction history: table with columns: Direction (badge in/out), Other party, Amount (colored +/-, right aligned), Memo, Date. Paging controls: Previous / Next + \"Page X of Y (N transactions)\" + page size selector maybe. Keep page size fixed at 10 with a select for 10/25/50? The API supports page_size. I'll add a simple page-size select.\n\nState: App holds token (localStorage), account (username, balance), transfers page state.\n\nAPI client (src/api.ts):\n\n```ts\nexport class ApiError extends Error {\n  status: number;\n}\n\nasync function request<T>(path, options): Promise<T>\n```\n\nToken stored in localStorage under \"bank.token\"; CSRF token under \"bank.csrf\".\n\nAfter login/register we get csrf_token; store it. Also fetch /api/auth/csrf on startup.\n\nLet me write the code now. Use TypeScript strict mode. Vite's react-ts template sets tsconfig; but there's no tsconfig.json in the project! Let me check: frontend has package.json, package-lock.json, index.html, vite.config.ts, src/main.tsx, src/App.tsx. No tsconfig.json and no @types/react, @types/react-dom in devDependencies! So `npm run build` runs `vite build` — Vite 8 with the react plugin; esbuild transpiles TSX without type checking. But without @types/react, the build might still work (esbuild doesn't need types) — but `React` imports... the new JSX transform doesn't need React import. Vite's react plugin uses automatic JSX runtime. So the build works without @types.\n\nBut for type checking (tsc), I need typescript + @types/react + @types/react-dom and a tsconfig. I should add these devDependencies and a tsconfig.json, plus a \"typecheck\" script. That's good practice and lets me verify types.\n\nLet me add:\n- devDependencies: typescript, @types/react, @types/react-dom\n- tsconfig.json with strict settings\n- scripts: \"build\": \"vite build\", \"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\", \"lint\": maybe eslint — skip eslint to avoid heavy deps; typecheck is enough. Hmm, \"run the lint and typecheck commands\" — if there's no lint command, typecheck suffices.\n\nAlso note npm ci in Docker requires package-lock.json to be in sync — I'll run `npm install` after adding deps, which updates the lock file.\n\nTailwind v4: install `tailwindcss` and `@tailwindcss/vite` (done). Add the plugin to vite.config.ts and create src/index.css.\n\nLet me check the installed versions and update vite.config.ts.\n\nOne important detail: Vite 8 + @tailwindcss/vite v4 compatibility. Let me verify the build works after configuring.\n\nLet me write all frontendfiles now.</think>\n\nBackend is solid. Now the frontend.","time":{"start":1794347243528,"end":1794347243551}}}
