{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794347536984,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c772923b001788SQAibeClwYT","messageID":"msg_1c7727fa5001Wjgke4SUV3Okk6","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794347536984,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c7729240001xzT3MhRarXzu2z","messageID":"msg_1c7727fa5001Wjgke4SUV3Okk6","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"text","text":"<think>The playwright driver failed due to a missing system library (libatomic.so.1). Installing system packages likely requires apt (no root? we are uid 1000). Let me check if apt is usable... Probably no root. Let me check `sudo -n true`.\n\nAlternatively, use jsdom for a DOM-level test. Let me try: install jsdom in a temp dir (not in the frontend project to avoid polluting package.json), write a small script that:\n1. Builds the app (already built to dist).\n2. Loads the built JS in jsdom with a fetch polyfill pointing to the running server (jsdom doesn't implement fetch; Node 24 has global fetch, and jsdom's window can use Node's fetch via a shim).\n3. Checks that the app renders the auth panel, then simulates login and checks the dashboard.\n\nThat's somewhat involved but doable. Since the built bundle is an ES module, jsdom can't easily execute it (jsdom doesn't support ES modules in script tags). Options: build with `vite build --mode` ... hmm. I could instead run the dev server and use a headless DOM approach...\n\nAlternative simpler runtime check: use React's server-side rendering to string (react-dom/server) with a jsdom-ish global to catch render-time errors. That validates component logic but not the fetch flows.\n\nHmm. Let me try to fix the missing library: check if `libatomic1` can be installed via apt-get (needs root). Let me check whether we have root: `id` showed uid=1000(node). Let me check `sudo -n true`.\n\nActually — playwright's node binary needs libatomic. Maybe there's another chrome build. Let's check if apt-get download works without root... `apt-get download libatomic1` then extract with dpkg -x to a local dir and set LD_LIBRARY_PATH. The apt sources might be reachable through a proxy. Let me test quickly.\n\nActually, is deep browser verification necessary? The core deliverable is the app. I'd like at least a smoke test that the React app mounts and renders without runtime errors. Let me try the apt approach with a short timeout; if it fails, fall back to a jsdom-based SSR-ish check.\n\nLet me check apt availability.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794347536960,"end":1794347536980}}}
