{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794347549401,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c772c2c4001FVw1OPUenf1EA2","messageID":"msg_1c7729954001NFNmJ2Osgo9tfq","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794347549402,"sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","part":{"id":"prt_1c772c2c8001kIdZkBjqi59FNm","messageID":"msg_1c7729954001NFNmJ2Osgo9tfq","sessionID":"ses_e38957fc2ffea5rGdpZf5rJULa","type":"text","text":"<think>No root, no apt. Let me try downloading the libatomic1 .deb directly from a Debian mirror through the proxy? The proxy is for pypi/npm registries only, so likely no general internet.\n\nLet me check if the npm registry could provide a chromium binary package, e.g. `@sparticuz/chromium` or `playwright-core` + `chromium` via `npm i chromium`? There's an npm package `chromium` that downloads a binary from a CDN (blocked). There's `puppeteer` which downloads Chrome from a CDN (blocked).\n\nAlternatively, npm package `@sparticuz/chromium` includes a brotli-compressed chromium binary in the npm package itself! It's ~50MB and designed for AWS Lambda (Amazon Linux). It might work on this system (glibc-based). But it also needs shared libs (libnss etc.) which are bundled in the package (sparticuz bundles the required .so files). This could work.\n\nHmm, this is a rabbit hole. Let me evaluate the cost/benefit: The frontend is a standard React app; the main risks are runtime errors and layout issues. A jsdom-based test can catch runtime errors in rendering and event handling. Let me do that instead — it's cheaper and quite effective.\n\nApproach: use jsdom (npm, dev-only in a scratch dir /tmp/opencode/frontend-check or in the workspace but not committed... Actually I could add jsdom as a devDependency and write a smoke test script in the frontend. That adds a dependency to package.json/package-lock — acceptable? It bloats the image's npm install (jsdom is dev-only but `npm ci` installs devDependencies in the Docker build stage). The Docker build would download jsdom (~50MB). Not ideal but harmless. Alternatively, install jsdom in a scratch dir outside the repo (/workspace/.scratch) and run the test from there with a script that imports the built bundle? The built bundle is ESM; jsdom can't run ESM scripts... but I can use jsdom's `runScripts: \"dangerously\"` with a bundled IIFE build! I can build an IIFE bundle with vite (lib mode) into a scratch dir, then load it in jsdom.\n\nPlan:\n1. In a scratch dir, `npm i jsdom` (network available).\n2. Use vite (from the frontend project) to build the app as an IIFE: `npx vite build --config scratch.vite.config.ts` with `build.lib` config → produces a JS file with no CSS import issues (css gets extracted separately; but the app's CSS import would be dropped in lib mode — fine for a smoke test).\n3. In the jsdom script: create a DOM with `<div id=\"root\">`, set up globals (fetch → node fetch to http://127.0.0.1:8000, localStorage, window.matchMedia...), inject the bundle, wait, then assert the DOM contains \"Sign in\" etc. Then simulate filling the form and clicking to log in, then check the balance appears and the transfer flow works.\n\nThat's a fair amount of work but gives real confidence in the frontend. jsdom supports form events (click, input). React 19 event delegation works in jsdom generally.\n\nLet me try it — but time-box it. If jsdom setup gets messy, I'll fall back to careful code review.\n\nActually, a lighter-weight alternative that catches most runtime errors: render the App with react-dom/server to string in a Node script using tsx/esbuild? The app uses localStorage and fetch in effects (useEffect doesn't run during SSR), so SSR would render the \"loading\" state only. Not very useful.\n\nLet me go with jsdom + IIFE bundle. Steps:\n\n1. mkdir /workspace/.scratch && cd .scratch && npm init -y && npm i jsdom\n2. Create vite.config for lib build:\n```ts\nimport react from \"@vitejs/plugin-react\";\nimport { defineConfig } from \"vite\";\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [react()],\n  build: {\n    lib: { entry: \"../frontend/src/main.tsx\", formats: [\"iife\"], name: \"App\", fileName: \"app\" },\n    outDir: \"../.scratch/dist\",\n    emptyOutDir: true,\n  },\n});\n```\nHmm, the entry's CSS import: in lib mode, CSS is emitted as a separate file — fine.\n\nBut `main.tsx` imports \"./index.css\" → vite lib build will emit a CSS file; the JS won't include CSS. Good for jsdom.\n\nThe base path for the bundle: iife format with name \"App\".\n\n3. jsdom script:\n```js\nconst { JSDOM } = require(\"jsdom\");\nconst dom = new JSDOM(`<!DOCTYPE html><div id=\"root\"></div>`, { url: \"http://localhost:8000/\", runScripts: \"dangerously\", pretendToBeVisual: true });\nconst { window } = dom;\nwindow.fetch = (input, init) => fetch(new URL(input, \"http://localhost:8000\"), init);  // node fetch\n// localStorage is supported by jsdom\nconst code = fs.readFileSync(\"dist/app.iife.js\", \"utf8\");\nconst script = new window.Function(code);  // or window.eval\nscript.call(window);\n```\nUsing `window.eval(code)` with runScripts: \"dangerously\" executes in the window context. Note: React 19 might use `queueMicrotask`, `MessageChannel` — jsdom supports those.\n\nThen wait a tick and check `window.document.body.textContent`.\n\nFor interactions: set input values via the native setter and dispatch \"input\" events (React 19 requires the native value setter trick):\n```js\nfunction setValue(el, value) {\n  const setter = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(window.HTMLInputElement.prototype, \"value\").set;\n  setter.call(el, value);\n  el.dispatchEvent(new window.Event(\"input\", { bubbles: true }));\n}\n```\nThen click the submit button: `button.dispatchEvent(new window.MouseEvent(\"click\", {bubbles: true}))` — React 19 attaches listeners to the root container, and events bubble → OK.\n\nForm submit: clicking a submit button inside a form in jsdom triggers form submission → jsdom doesn't implement form submission navigation but does fire the submit event. React's onSubmit handler should fire. Good.\n\nLet me write this. It's a solid verification of the whole frontend flow.\n\nLet me do it in /workspace/.scratch (gitignored? The repo has a .gitignore? Let me check. There's no .gitignore in the workspace listing! Let me check git status later — I should not commit scratch files. Actually the task doesn't require committing at all. But I should keep the repo clean. Let me check .gitignore existence.\n\nLet me proceed: create the scratch dir outside the repo? /tmp/opencode isn't writable (permission denied earlier). Let me check again: /tmp/opencode is owned by root with drwxr-xr-x → not writable by uid 1000. But /tmp is world-writable (drwxrwxrwt). So I can create /tmp/scratch. Let me use /tmp/bank-check.\n\nLet me set itup.</think>\n\nLet me set up a browser-less DOM smoke test to verify the frontend actually works.","time":{"start":1794347549384,"end":1794347549398}}}
